::ALG2_U02_L04_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What are the zeros of f(x) = (x - 2)(x + 7)? {
~x = -2 and x = 7 #The signs are reversed.
=x = 2 and x = -7 #Correct. Set each factor equal to 0.
~x = 0 and x = 7 #x = 0 is not from these factors.
~x = 2 and x = 7 #Only x = 2 is correct.
}

::ALG2_U02_L04_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the multiplicity of x = -4 in f(x) = (x + 4)<sup>3</sup>? {
~1 #The exponent is not 1.
~2 #The exponent is not 2.
~4 #This is the opposite of the zero value.
=3 #Correct. The factor repeats 3 times.
}

::ALG2_U02_L04_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A zero with even multiplicity usually does what? {
=touches and turns #Correct. Even multiplicity usually touches and turns.
~crosses sharply #That is typical of odd multiplicity.
~has no x-intercept #It is still an x-intercept.
~becomes a y-intercept #It remains an x-intercept.
}

::ALG2_U02_L04_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A zero with odd multiplicity usually does what? {
~touches only #This is typical of even multiplicity.
~has no graph behavior #Zeros affect x-axis behavior.
=crosses the x-axis #Correct. Odd multiplicity usually crosses.
~turns into a vertex #Not necessarily.
}

::ALG2_U02_L04_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which zero comes from x - 9? {
=x = 9 #Correct. x - 9 = 0 gives x = 9.
~x = -9 #The sign is reversed.
~x = 0 #The factor is not x.
~x = 1 #This does not make the factor 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L04_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L04_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What are the zeros of f(x) = (x - 2)(x + 7)? {
~x = -2 and x = 7 #The signs are reversed.
